A transaction writes its journal blocks in runs

The replay and checkpoint paths read and write the log in runs; the commit
path that fills it did not. Every journal block went out through the cache
carrying BC_TMP and BC_FLUSH -- exactly the pair that makes
ext4_bcache_free flush on release -- so each was a 4 KiB device command
issued from inside the loop building the transaction.

Measured before: 2,000 small files holding about 20 KB of content cost
27,930 device writes and 100 MB, and on a 500-file run 558 of 1,492
commands landed inside the journal.

A transaction's journal blocks are consecutive by construction
(jbd_journal_alloc_block hands out journal->last++), and the journal file
is laid down as a long run, so they are physically contiguous too. The
payload is now gathered and issued one command per run, bypassing the
cache: nothing reads a journal block between writing it and the commit,
the cache would only flush it per block anyway, and any copy left from an
earlier lap of the ring is invalidated once the new bytes are down.

After, on the same fixtures: journal commands 558 -> 192, total 1,492 ->
1,126, and the size histogram gains 24-36 KB writes where everything used
to be 4 KiB. The remaining single-sector writes are the file payloads
themselves.

Ordering is unchanged and is the reason the flush sits at the end of
jbd_journal_prepare: the caller barriers next, and a barrier orders only
what has already been handed to the medium. A NULL batch falls back to the
per-block path, so memory pressure costs speed rather than correctness.
The allocator's "0 means the ring is full" convention is now honoured here
too -- it was previously passed to jbd_block_get_noread, which refuses it.

Gates for a commit-path change: crash consistency 203 cut points, 203
recovered by the Linux kernel; reordered writes 236 cut points, 0 failed,
with the barriers-ignored negative control still failing; revoke records,
error injection, replay speed and the Linux differential all passing.
